Host binding:


- 🔥We often need to automatically add a CSS class or style, or bind a specific DOM property on
    the host element of a directive or component. 🔥The host metadata, as we just saw, allow to
    declare event bindings on the host element(previous section). 🔥🔥We can use the same host
    metadata to also declare property bindings.

    💡Let’s say we want to add a specific CSS class (is-required) to a label if the associated field
    is required. Maybe this class adds a nice marker next to the label, or a small asterisk -
    that’s not really important. This will not validate the field in any way - it will simply grab
    the form state, and use this result to style the label.

    This is, again, a task that perfectly fits a directive:

        @Directive({
            selector: 'label[nsAddClassIfRequired]',
        })
        export class AddClassIfRequired {
        }

    We will use it in a signal form:
        
        <label for="firstName" 👉[nsAddClassIfRequired]="userForm.firstName">First name</label>
        <input id="firstName" [formField]="userForm.firstName" />
    
    🔥We then need to grab a reference to the state of the field in our directive. With signal forms,
    each field is represented by a FieldTree(remember from chapter 21, section 2 😉).

        @Directive({
            selector: 'label[nsAddClassIfRequired]',
        })
        export class AddClassIfRequired {
          👉readonly fieldTree = input.required<FieldTree<unknown>>({ alias: 'nsAddClassIfRequired' });
        }
    
    Now that we have a reference to the FieldTree, 🔥it’s easy to know if the field is required, by
    reading its current state and calling required(). 🔥The last step requires us to add the class
    is-required to our host element if that’s the case. That’s where the host property binding
    enters the scene! Once again, the syntax is almost identical to the syntaxdoes html label 
    we would use in a template:

        host: {
            '[prop]': 'expression'
        }

    🔥🔥And that would automatically update the host’s property prop, every time the expression
    changes.


    In our case, we can define a computed signal that returns true or false, depending on whether
    the field is currently required.

        @Directive({
            selector: 'label[nsAddClassIfRequired]',
            host: {
                '[class.is-required]': 'isRequired()' 👈
            }
        })
        export class AddClassIfRequired {
            readonly fieldTree = input.required<FieldTree<unknown>>({ alias: 'nsAddClassIfRequired' });
         👉 protected readonly isRequired = computed(() => {
                const fieldTree = this.fieldTree();
                const state = fieldTree();
                return state.required();
            });
        }


- ⚠️Note that the directive we built has a custom selector, 🔥🔥but if you decide that you want
    to apply these directives to every label, you can change their selector to label, and they will
    automatically be applied on every label of your application.

    Currently our selector:

        selector: 'label[nsAddClassIfRequired]'

    ✅so Angular applies it only here:
        
        <label for="firstName" 👉[nsAddClassIfRequired]="userForm.firstName">First name</label>

    ❌But not here:

        <label>LastName</label>
    
    But if we change the selector into label,

        selector: 'label'
    
    ✅then, these would all get the directive automatically:

        <label>FirstName</label>
        <label>LastName</label>
        <label>Email</label>
        <label>Password</label>



- 😮These few lines of code are really powerful: every time the directive is used in a form, Angular
    will automatically add or remove our custom class depending on the current field configuration
    or state!

- 🔥It can be used to bind other kinds of properties, not just CSS classes. For example, some
    component libraries use it to add accessibility attributes (aria.xxx) to a host element.

- Another way to achieve the same thing is to decorate a property with @HostBinding. This was the
    recommended way in previous versions of Angular, ✅but the host metadata are now the preferred
    way